  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m runs in the browser as a plain script (no module system), while [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS Node. A single file
  that both can consume needs a UMD-style wrapper ([38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m if it exists, else attach to [38;5;153mwindow[39m), which is real
  boilerplate for a 5-line config. A browser-only file needs none of that but can't be [38;5;153mrequire[39md from [38;5;153msrc/[39m later without
  changing it.

  - [1mBrowser-only[0m — a plain [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m at the repo root next to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, loaded by a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before it. Simplest thing
    that works. Costs: if [38;5;153msrc/[39m ever needs the endpoint, you revisit the format.
  - [1mShared browser + Node[0m — UMD wrapper so both sides can use it. Buys future reuse from [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Costs: boilerplate now
    for a consumer that doesn't exist; [38;5;153msrc/[39m currently holds no config at all.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-only.[0m Nothing in [38;5;153msrc/[39m touches the API today, and YAGNI applies — the upgrade to shared is a
  few lines if it's ever actually needed.
